//@version=6
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ICT Macros
// Author: Filipe Teixeira Amaral
// Version: 1.0
//
// Description:
// The ICT macro windows as first-class chart objects: each window bracketed by
// a vertical line at its start and one at its end, the pair joined by a
// captioned rectangle on a strip at the chart's edge. The caption carries the
// window's name with a countdown under it -- to the open while the window is
// ahead, of what is left once inside. Split out of Time-Based Vertical Lines
// the way the NinjaTrader pair is: that tool keeps the plain time lines, this
// one owns the windows, and the two share a chart without double-booking an
// edge -- the band strip defaults to the bottom and the line captions to the
// top.
//
// The slots are the full standard macro map, in New York time. The rule is
// :50 to :10 around every top of the hour -- twenty minutes straddling it --
// except the final trading hour, which replaces its window with the Final
// Hour (15:15 - 15:45) and Market On Close (15:45 - 16:00) specials; the
// settlement portion (15:50 - 16:00) rides inside MOC as its own optional
// slot. That gives one row per window from the 18:50 Asia macro around the
// clock to the close, grouped by the session each falls in. The playbook
// trades the six regular-hours windows plus the Final Hour and MOC, so
// exactly that set starts enabled; every other session's windows are carried
// but off. The 08:30 news release and the opening ranges are separate
// events, not macros -- the 08:30 line lives in Time-Based Vertical Lines.
//
// Features:
// - 24 macro slots, one row each: on/off, start and end as HH:MM, label
// - Empty label captions the band with the window's times; the Final Hour,
//   MOC and Settlement windows come pre-labeled
// - Countdown on its own line under the window's name
// - A window may cross midnight (23:50 - 00:10 does); it stays keyed to the
//   day it starts on
// - Bands and captions ride a strip at the bottom (or top) edge, set clear
//   of the bars by an adjustable gap
// - Bracket lines can switch off, leaving only the bands on the strip
// - Every window drawn for the whole day, future ones included
// - Time zone support and historical-days control
//
// Notes:
// - Times are read as HH:MM in the selected zone. An entry that is not a
//   valid time is skipped, with a note in the Pine Logs pane; an end before
//   the start means the window runs into the next day.
// - The strip hangs off the visible bars, not the panel edge: a script only
//   knows prices, never pixels, so it makes room by having the chart's
//   auto-scale give up range under (or over) the candles. Pine has no way to
//   read the viewport's own bounds, so the strip cannot be pinned to the
//   panel edge the way a platform-native tool could pin it.
// - A window later today sits past the last bar. TradingView draws at most
//   500 bars past the last one, and the chart only reveals as much of that
//   as the right margin allows (Chart settings > Appearance).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

indicator('ICT Macros', overlay = true, max_lines_count = 500, max_boxes_count = 500)

MS_PER_DAY = 86400000
HALF_DAY_MS = 43200000
FALLBACK_LOOKBACK = 100

// Distinct days kept in the log: comfortably more than the five the history
// cutoff can ask for, so the oldest day still wanted is always still in there.
MAX_TRACKED_DAYS = 20

//==============================================================================
// INPUTS
//==============================================================================
GRP_GENERAL = 'General Settings'
GRP_BAND = 'Band'
GRP_ASIA = 'Asia Macros'
GRP_LONDON = 'London Macros'
GRP_PREMARKET = 'NY Pre-Market Macros'
GRP_AM = 'NY AM Macros'
GRP_LUNCH = 'NY Lunch Macros'
GRP_PM = 'NY PM Macros'
GRP_FINAL = 'Final Hour Macros'

tz = input.string('America/New_York', 'Time Zone', options = ['America/New_York', 'America/Chicago', 'America/Denver', 'America/Los_Angeles', 'Europe/London', 'Europe/Paris', 'Europe/Berlin', 'Europe/Moscow', 'Asia/Tokyo', 'Asia/Shanghai', 'Asia/Hong_Kong', 'Asia/Singapore', 'Asia/Kolkata', 'Australia/Sydney', 'Pacific/Auckland', 'GMT-12', 'GMT-11', 'GMT-10', 'GMT-9', 'GMT-8', 'GMT-7', 'GMT-6', 'GMT-5', 'GMT-4', 'GMT-3', 'GMT-2', 'GMT-1', 'GMT', 'GMT+1', 'GMT+2', 'GMT+3', 'GMT+4', 'GMT+5', 'GMT+6', 'GMT+7', 'GMT+8', 'GMT+9', 'GMT+10', 'GMT+11', 'GMT+12'], tooltip = 'Zone every window time below is read in.', group = GRP_GENERAL)

history_days = input.int(1, 'Days of History to Show', 0, 5, tooltip = 'How far back to keep drawing windows, counted from the last day the chart has bars for -- that day always keeps its own. 0 = that day and today.', group = GRP_GENERAL)

include_tomorrow = input.bool(false, 'Include Tomorrow', tooltip = 'Also draw tomorrow\'s windows. Useful late in the session, when everything for today has already happened. Only worth it from about the 5-minute timeframe up: TradingView draws at most 500 bars past the last one.', group = GRP_GENERAL)

show_bracket_lines = input.bool(true, 'Show Bracket Lines', tooltip = 'The vertical lines at each window\'s start and end. Off leaves only the rectangles on the strip.', group = GRP_GENERAL)

line_width = input.int(1, 'Line Width', 1, 5, tooltip = 'Width of the bracket lines and the band outline.', group = GRP_GENERAL)

macro_color = input.color(#F57C00, 'Color', inline = 'stroke', group = GRP_GENERAL)
macro_style = input.string('Dotted', 'Style', options = ['Solid', 'Dashed', 'Dotted'], inline = 'stroke', group = GRP_GENERAL)

show_captions = input.bool(true, 'Show Captions', tooltip = 'Print each window\'s caption on its band.', group = GRP_BAND)

caption_size = input.string('small', 'Caption Size', options = ['tiny', 'small', 'normal', 'large'], group = GRP_BAND)

show_countdown = input.bool(true, 'Countdown', tooltip = 'Add a line under the window\'s name: the time to its open while it is ahead, what is left of it once inside.', group = GRP_BAND)

strip_position = input.string('Bottom', 'Strip Position', options = ['Bottom', 'Top'], tooltip = 'Which edge of the chart the strip of bands rides. Bottom leaves the top free for Time-Based Vertical Lines\' captions, so the two tools share a chart without double-booking an edge.', group = GRP_BAND)

edge_gap_percent = input.float(3.0, 'Edge Gap (%)', 0, 40, step = 0.5, tooltip = 'Clear space between the bars and the strip, as a share of the visible price range. The chart gives up that much scale to make the room, so a larger gap means slightly flatter candles. The strip hangs off the visible bars -- Pine cannot read the panel\'s own bounds to pin it there.', group = GRP_BAND)

band_height_percent = input.float(4.0, 'Band Height (%)', 0, 40, step = 0.5, tooltip = 'Thickness of the rectangle joining a window\'s two lines, as a share of the visible price range. The caption rides its centre and spills past a narrow rectangle rather than being cut off, so this is about how the band looks, not whether the text fits. 0 draws only the bracket lines.', group = GRP_BAND)

band_transp = input.int(85, 'Band Transparency', 0, 100, group = GRP_BAND)

// One row per window of the standard macro map, grouped by session. They are
// inputs rather than constants because the window a desk actually trades
// drifts from the published one, and because the rows serve any other
// recurring span just as well.
MACRO_TOOLTIP = 'On/off, the window\'s start and end as HH:MM in the selected zone, then its label. An empty label captions the band with the window\'s times; an end before the start rolls into the next day.'

